icient offenses in the league so far, ranking in the top eight in four of the five main efficiency measures. They have thrown more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score has not called for it. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the 2nd lowest pre-snap motion rate in the league. Dak Prescott has run the passing game and leaned heavily on CeeDee Lamb, who has drawn 32% of the targets.","defense":"The Cowboys have one of the worst defenses in the league this season, ranking 27th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. Even its strongest area, sack rate, only ranks 14th, and it is 32nd in EPA allowed per rush. Quinnen Williams leads the team with 8 sacks, and James Houston has added 3.5 despite playing only 34% of the snaps. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a blitz rate that ranks 4th lowest in the league.","special":"The Cowboys have been one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 52% of their short-yardage chances so far. Their kickers have made 80% of field goals, which is well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 19% of the money on the books. Their linebackers were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Dak Prescott has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 3,049 yards. CeeDee Lamb has been the clear top target with 32% of the looks and 1,108 receiving yards. Javonte Williams has handled most of the work in the backfield with 74% of the carries. On defense, Quinnen Williams leads the team with 8 sacks.","coaching":"Brian Schottenheimer is calling the offense as head coach and Christian Parker is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2022 Seahawks and the 2022 Broncos. Their defense looks most like the 2025 Seahawks under Mike Macdonald and the 2023 Bears.","trends":"The offense went from 5th in EPA per play in 2025 to 6th so far this season, about where it was. The defense moved from 32nd in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 30th so far this season, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was pre-snap motion rate, which went from 59% to 47%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}